\ ******************************************************************
\ *	qdisc.6502 - a DFS-free, non-blocking sector reader for the
\ *	Master's WD1770: the RESIDENT half
\ ******************************************************************
\ *	beeb-port-kit, MIT, Kieran Connell 2026. Baron syntax, plain 6502.
\ *	MASTER ONLY (the registers are the Master's &FE24-&FE2B).
\ *
\ *	WHAT IT IS. The kit's THIRD answer to "does the game read the disc
\ *	after it starts" (loader.6502's contract has the other two: keep
\ *	the filing system and own the machine, or release it and never load
\ *	again). Here the game releases the filing system (*FX 140,0,
\ *	loader.6502's release_fs) after its boot loads AND KEEPS READING
\ *	THE DISC, by driving the 1770 itself: a short self-modifying routine
\ *	at &0D00 stores each byte the controller hands over by NMI, and
\ *	every call returns AT ONCE - the caller polls qd_busy, or calls
\ *	qd_wait. So no OS call is made after the release, the DFS's
\ *	workspace is the game's (&0E00 up, HAZEL &C000-&C2FF), and a read
\ *	can run in the background while the game plays.
\ *	  qdisc.6502       this file: seek, read, busy, wait, warm.
\ *	  qdisc_init.6502  BOOT-ONLY: the NMI routine's source, qd_cfg (the
\ *	                   drive and step rate, asked of the DFS and CMOS
\ *	                   BEFORE *FX 140) and qd_init (patch, install,
\ *	                   restore to track 0).
\ *	  qdload.6502      files by NAME: load_stream's contract without
\ *	                   the DFS, out of a catalogue cached at boot.
\ *	  qdload_init.6502 BOOT-ONLY: qd_boot, which reads the catalogue.
\ *	A port that only wants raw sectors takes the first two.
\ *
\ *	WHERE IT CAME FROM.
\ *	  The 1942 port (github.com/kieranhj/1942-beeb), src/lib/qdisc.6502,
\ *	  src/lib/qdisc_init.6502, src/qdcfg.6502 - every seam load of that
\ *	  game from 2026-09-24 to v1.0, and its rationed background reads
\ *	  (bgread.6502) during play.
\ *	  And before that scarybeasts' quicdisc (misc/quicdisc/quicdisc.asm
\ *	  in his beebjit tree), whose approach this is: *FX 140,0 takes the
\ *	  DFS off the NMI, a routine at &0D00 stores the bytes, the call
\ *	  returns at once. RELICENSED FOR THIS USE with his blessing,
\ *	  2026-10-12: "relicense / rewrite etc. the quicdisc stuff however
\ *	  is useful". Keep this attribution when you fork the file.
\ *	What is different from quicdisc, and why:
\ *	  - MASTER ONLY: the 8271 and the Model B's &FE84 1770 are gone, so
\ *	    the registers are constants;
\ *	  - A RUN OF SECTORS, not a whole track: qd_read takes the first
\ *	    sector and a page count, and FORCE INTERRUPT (&D0, which raises
\ *	    no completion interrupt) ends the multi-sector read at the count;
\ *	  - THE ROUTINE IS DISARMED BETWEEN READS. On an Acorn 1770 board
\ *	    the NMI line is DRQ OR INTRQ (jsbeeb's wd-fdc.js, from beebjit),
\ *	    so a SEEK's completion interrupt runs it too - and quicdisc's
\ *	    version then stores one junk byte after the buffer. Here its
\ *	    first byte is RTI (&40) while no read runs and STA zp (&85)
\ *	    while one does;
\ *	  - THE DRIVE AND THE STEP RATE ARE THE MACHINE'S (1942 BUGS.md #85,
\ *	    a real drive, 2026-10-11): qd_cfg asks the DFS which drive the
\ *	    game was run from and the CMOS for *CONFIGURE FDRIVE, and qd_init
\ *	    patches every drive select, restore and seek below. Drive 0 at
\ *	    6 ms always hung on drive 0 and read garbage on drive 1; a Gotek
\ *	    takes any rate, so only a real drive showed it.
\ *
\ *	WHAT WAS MEASURED, and when:
\ *	  - lib/test/qdisc_test.mjs (jsbeeb Master, headless): every track of
\ *	    both sides of a patterned double-sided disc byte-exact, a run
\ *	    inside a track, the sentinel after every buffer untouched with a
\ *	    SEEK between reads. The header of that file has the figures.
\ *	  - 35 CYCLES A BYTE on the common path, COUNTED (NMI entry 7, sta zp
\ *	    3, lda 4, sta 4, inc 6, beq 2, lda zp 3, rti 6), +18 on a page's
\ *	    last byte. A single-density byte arrives every 64 us = 128 cycles,
\ *	    so a read leaves ~73% of the CPU to the caller.
\ *	  - A READ COMMAND WITH E SET (&94) SETTLES THE HEAD 15-30 ms ON A
\ *	    REAL 1770, EVERY TIME, motor running or not (1942, KC's Master,
\ *	    2026-10-10). jsbeeb up to 2.3.x settles only after a spin-up
\ *	    (fixed upstream, jsbeeb PR #1226), so no emulator run shows it.
\ *	    qd_read issues QD_READM = &94 because qdload.6502 seeks before
\ *	    every run; a reader that reads track after track on one track
\ *	    without seeking can patch qd_read_op + 1 to &90 and save it.
\ *	  - A BUSY 1770 SILENTLY IGNORES A COMMAND ("command while busy -
\ *	    ignoring", jsbeeb). Wait for idle (qd_wait) before every one.
\ *	  - THE TARGET MUST STAY PUT FOR THE WHOLE READ: the NMI stores
\ *	    through an absolute address with whatever ROMSEL, ANDY and ACCCON
\ *	    the interrupted code has. Read into main RAM below &3000, or hold
\ *	    the paging still until qd_busy says done.
\ *	  - SEI DOES NOT MASK IT. The NMI is the NMI: a read runs on through
\ *	    a masked section, which is how a load under SEI works at all.
\ *
\ *	THE REGISTERS (Master): &FE24 drive control, &FE28 command/status,
\ *	&FE29 track, &FE2A sector, &FE2B data. Drive control &25 = drive 0,
\ *	not in reset, single density (DFS discs are FM); +&10 is side 1.
\ *	Not measured: MOS 3.5 / the Compact, a 1772 board (the step rate
\ *	clamp is for one: qdisc_init.6502), MMFS, ADFS-formatted discs.
\ *
\ *	THE INCLUDER DEFINES, before this file is included:
\ *	  QD_NMI_A   one zero-page byte, the NMI routine's saved A. 1942 used
\ *	             &A0: &A0-&A7 are the MOS's NMI-owner workspace, and after
\ *	             *FX 140,0 the NMI owner is this (measured unwritten by
\ *	             anything else through a whole game, 1942 2026-10-11).
\ *	             Never a Baron ZA_AUTO: the NMI can fire inside any code.
\ *	And it OWNS &0D00-&0D31 (QD_NMI): the NMI routine and its page count.
\ *	Call nothing here before qd_init (qdisc_init.6502).
\ *
\ *	Fork this into your project; keep this header.
\ ******************************************************************

ASSERT QD_NMI_A >= 0 AND QD_NMI_A < &100   ; a zero-page byte: STA zp is the switch

QD_CTRL  = &FE24
QD_CMD   = &FE28                ; write: command; read: status (clears INTRQ)
QD_TRK   = &FE29
QD_SEC   = &FE2A
QD_DATA  = &FE2B
QD_SIDE0 = &25                  ; drive 0, not reset, single density
QD_SIDE  = &10                  ; drive control's side bit
QD_RESTORE = &00                ; restore to track 0, spin-up, 6 ms steps
QD_SEEK    = &10                ; seek to the data register's track
QD_READM   = &94                ; read sectors, MULTIPLE, spin-up, E: settle
QD_ABORT   = &D0                ; force interrupt: no completion NMI

QD_NMI       = &0D00            ; the routine, copied here by qd_init
QD_NMI_PAGES = QD_NMI + &31     ; pages still to come
QD_NMI_ST    = QD_NMI + 6       ; the store's operand, the running pointer
QD_ARMED     = &85              ; STA zp - the routine's first byte while reading
QD_IDLE      = &40              ; RTI

\ ---- qd_seek: A = track. Returns at once; poll qd_busy. ------------
\ THE LABELLED OPERANDS (qd_seek_op, qd_ctl0, qd_ctl1, qd_warm_op, and
\ qd_init_ctl / qd_rest_op in qdisc_init.6502) ARE PATCHED by qd_init from
\ what qd_cfg found. The constants are drive 0 at 6 ms.
.qd_seek
    sta QD_DATA
    lda QD_CMD                  ; clear any INTRQ, so the next one is an edge
.qd_seek_op
    lda #QD_SEEK
    sta QD_CMD
    jmp qd_settle

\ ---- qd_read: X pages from sector A of the current track into (qd_dst),
\ on side Y (0 = the side the game was run from, 1 = the other). Returns at
\ once; poll qd_busy. X is 1..10, and a run may not cross the end of the
\ track: the caller splits it (qdload.6502's qd_sectors does).
.qd_read
    sta QD_SEC
    stx QD_NMI_PAGES
    lda qd_dst
    sta QD_NMI_ST
    lda qd_dst + 1
    sta QD_NMI_ST + 1
.qd_ctl0
    lda #QD_SIDE0               ; (patched: the boot's drive and side)
    cpy #0
    beq qd_read_s0
.qd_ctl1
    lda #QD_SIDE0 EOR QD_SIDE   ; (patched: the same drive's other side)
.qd_read_s0
    sta QD_CTRL
    lda #QD_ARMED
    sta QD_NMI
    lda QD_CMD                  ; clear any INTRQ
.qd_read_op
    lda #QD_READM
    sta QD_CMD
\ falls into qd_settle
\ ---- the 1770 wants ~48 us before BUSY reads true after a command ---
.qd_settle
{
    ldx #20
    .loop
    dex
    bne loop
    rts
}

\ ---- qd_busy: A = the status's BUSY bit (1 while a command runs), Z
\ clear while busy. Reading the status clears INTRQ.
.qd_busy
    lda QD_CMD
    and #1
    rts

\ ---- qd_wait: until the 1770 is idle; returns its status in A -------
\ (bits 2-4: lost data, CRC error, record not found).
.qd_wait
{
    .loop
    lda QD_CMD
    lsr a
    bcs loop
    lda QD_CMD
    rts
}

\ ---- qd_warm: keep the drive's motor on --------------------------
\ A seek to the track the head is already on: it returns at once, spins
\ the motor up if it had stopped, and restarts the 1770's own idle count -
\ it turns the motor off after ~10 idle revolutions, 2 s. Call it a little
\ before a load you can see coming, so the load does not pay the spin-up
\ (1942's drive_warm: a set seam held 2.87 M cycles against the DFS's
\ 6.75 M). Its completion interrupt meets the DISARMED NMI routine.
\ Does nothing if a command is running. Clobbers A.
.qd_warm
    lda QD_CMD                  ; the status: clears any INTRQ
    lsr a
    bcs qd_warm_out             ; busy: spinning anyway
    lda QD_TRK
    sta QD_DATA
.qd_warm_op
    lda #QD_SEEK                ; (patched: the step rate)
    sta QD_CMD
.qd_warm_out
    rts

.qd_dst  EQUW 0                 ; qd_read's destination
